            <ArticleTitle>Diabetes is not sufficient justification for IIb/IIIa use in percutaneous coronary intervention.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>376-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ccd.26130</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diabetics, particularly insulin treated, have increased risk of ischemic events and bleeding with percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). The relative risk of atherothrombotic versus bleeding events is higher in diabetics than non-diabetics. Bivalirudin with provisional IIb/IIIa therapy has less bleeding and lower one-year mortality than routine IIb/IIIa therapy in diabetics undergoing PCI. IIb/IIIa agents should be reserved for provisional and niche applications during PCI.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
